import{J as e,n as t,ot as n,q as r}from"./chunks/framework.DcNbVdPy.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Describe a dimension as a Java level stem, and keep one World per dimension","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"Describe a dimension as a Java level stem, and keep one World per dimension"}],["meta",{"property":"og:description","content":""}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://jsprismarine.dev/adr/0004-data-driven-dimensions"}],["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://jsprismarine.dev/adr/0004-data-driven-dimensions"}]],"editSource":{"kind":"file","url":"https://github.com/JSPrismarine/JSPrismarine/edit/master/docs/adr/0004-data-driven-dimensions.md"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0004-data-driven-dimensions.md","filePath":"adr/0004-data-driven-dimensions.md","lastUpdated":1793660130000}`),a={name:`adr/0004-data-driven-dimensions.md`};function o(t,i,a,o,s,c){return n(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<div><h1 id="describe-a-dimension-as-a-java-level-stem-and-keep-one-world-per-dimension" tabindex="-1">Describe a dimension as a Java level stem, and keep one <code>World</code> per dimension <a class="header-anchor" href="#describe-a-dimension-as-a-java-level-stem-and-keep-one-world-per-dimension" aria-label="Permalink to “Describe a dimension as a Java level stem, and keep one World per dimension”">​</a></h1><p>Status: accepted</p><p>Every world this server loads is the Overworld. <code>WorldManager.buildWorld</code> never sets the <code>dimension</code> option, <code>GeneratorManager</code> builds one instance of each class in <code>Generators.ts</code> and cannot configure it per dimension, <code>DataDrivenGenerator</code> is typed to <code>MultiNoiseBiomeSource</code> so the End cannot be built, the LevelDB provider is bound to one <code>World</code> per <code>db/</code> where Bedrock writes three dimensions into one, and <code>Player.setWorld</code> sends a fixed Overworld, Nether, Nether, Overworld sequence so the client always ends in dimension 0. We adopt Java&#39;s model as the internal one: a <code>DimensionType</code> loaded from <code>dimension_type</code> JSON with a three-row Bedrock overlay, a <code>LevelStem</code> per dimension taken from a <code>world_preset</code> and overridable in config, a <code>GeneratorFactories</code> registry keyed by generator kind id, and one <code>Level</code> per <code>worlds.&lt;name&gt;</code> entry that owns the seed, the LevelDB directory and one <code>World</code> per stem. <code>World</code> stays the per-dimension runtime. Portals and the client&#39;s dimension change sequence are built on those types.</p><p>The research is in <a href="/pr-preview/pr-3029/research/data-driven-dimensions.html">data-driven-dimensions.md</a>, <a href="/pr-preview/pr-3029/research/dimension-storage-network-config.html">dimension-storage-network-config.md</a> and <a href="/pr-preview/pr-3029/research/portals-and-dimension-travel.html">portals-and-dimension-travel.md</a>. The implementation is sliced in <a href="/pr-preview/pr-3029/plans/data-driven-dimensions.html">docs/plans/data-driven-dimensions.md</a>.</p><h2 id="what-we-build" tabindex="-1">What we build <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-we-build" aria-label="Permalink to “What we build”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Java&#39;s three data objects, parsed in <code>@jsprismarine/worldgen</code>.</strong> A <code>dimension_type</code> is the physics of a dimension: <code>min_y</code>, <code>height</code>, <code>logical_height</code>, <code>coordinate_scale</code>, light, skybox, clock and the <code>attributes</code> map that 1.21.11 moved the old booleans into. A level stem binds a type to a generator. A generator is a tagged union on <code>type</code>: <code>minecraft:noise</code> with <code>settings</code> and a <code>biome_source</code>, <code>minecraft:flat</code> with layers, <code>minecraft:debug</code>. The vanilla datapack ships its three stems only inside the seven <code>worldgen/world_preset</code> files, so <code>parseWorldPreset</code> is the entry point and <code>parseBiomeSource</code> is the missing dispatch over the four biome source classes the package already has. <code>@jsprismarine/packs</code> routes <code>dimension_type</code>, <code>dimension</code> and the <code>worldgen/*</code> registries into <code>Definitions</code>, which is what lets a user datapack add a dimension, and what retires the private cache walker in <code>javaWorldgenData.ts</code>.</p><p><strong>A Bedrock overlay of three rows in <code>@jsprismarine/mapping</code>.</strong> Java id to Bedrock id, Bedrock name (<code>minecraft:nether</code> for <code>minecraft:the_nether</code>), the client height clamp, and the <code>Generator</code> enum value for <code>StartGame</code>. The Nether row carries <code>height 128</code>: Java&#39;s type says 256 with <code>logical_height 128</code>, the Bedrock client discards or crashes on sub chunks above 128, and writing the clamp as data is safer than deriving it from <code>logical_height</code>. A type with no row is custom, gets an id from 1000 up in stem order, and is announced through <code>DimensionDataPacket</code>.</p><p><strong><code>DimensionType</code>, <code>GeneratorFactories</code> and <code>Level</code> in <code>@jsprismarine/prismarine</code>.</strong><code>DimensionType</code> replaces <code>DimensionDefinition</code> and carries the Java definition, the Bedrock id and name, the Bedrock-effective bounds and a <code>custom</code> flag. <code>GeneratorFactories</code> replaces <code>GeneratorManager</code>: a factory is <code>{ kind, build(context) }</code> where the context is the stem, the dimension type, the seed, the content and the worldgen registries, and the built-in kinds are <code>minecraft:noise</code> over <code>DataDrivenGenerator</code>, <code>minecraft:flat</code> rewritten to honor its settings and the dimension&#39;s <code>minY</code>, <code>jsprismarine:void</code>, and <code>minecraft:debug</code> registered so the preset parses and throwing &quot;not supported&quot; at build time. A <code>Level</code> is one <code>worlds.&lt;name&gt;</code> entry: name, seed, provider handle, resolved stems, the clock, game rules, weather and the player roster. It builds one <code>World</code> per stem, lazily by default and eagerly when the config says <code>load: &#39;eager&#39;</code>.</p><p><strong>One provider per <code>Level</code>.</strong> <code>readChunk(dimension, cx, cz, ...)</code> and <code>listChunks(dimension)</code> take the dimension; <code>writeChunk</code> reads <code>chunk.getDimension()</code>. <code>level.dat</code> gains <code>Generator</code>, <code>FlatWorldLayers</code>, <code>NetherScale</code> and <code>Time</code>; player records gain Bedrock&#39;s <code>DimensionId</code> and <code>SpawnDimension</code>; entity NBT gains <code>DimensionId</code> and <code>PortalCooldown</code>. The resolved stems persist in the existing <code>jsprismarine:world_data</code> record in the <code>world_gen_settings</code> shape, and on a mismatch with the config the saved stems win and the server logs the difference, because a world keeps the terrain it was generated with.</p><p><strong>One worker pool per server.</strong> <code>noiseSettings</code>, <code>biomeParameters</code> and the biome source move from pool setup into task input, the worker keys its compiled pipeline by settings and seed instead of seed alone, and <code>ShardedGenerationPool.shardFor</code> hashes the dimension id so two dimensions&#39; chunk (0, 0) do not always share a shard. Three eager pools of six workers would cost three times the roughly 500 MiB ADR 0002 budgets for one.</p><p><strong>Config as preset plus overrides.</strong> <code>worlds.&lt;name&gt;</code> becomes <code>{ seed, provider, preset?, dimensions?: { &#39;&lt;id&gt;&#39;: { type?, generator?, load? } } }</code>. <code>preset</code> defaults to <code>minecraft:normal</code>. The old <code>generator: &#39;Overworld&#39; | &#39;Flat&#39;</code> and <code>layers</code> keys are translated once, logged as deprecated, and rewritten on the next save.</p><p><strong>The client sequence Dragonfly, Geyser and Nukkit agree on.</strong> One <code>ChangeDimension</code> with the real target id, <code>StopSound</code>, <code>NetworkChunkPublisherUpdate</code>, a server-sent <code>PlayerAction ChangeDimensionAck</code>, the arrival chunks, <code>PlayStatus PlayerSpawn</code>, then wait for <code>ServerboundLoadingScreen EndLoadingScreen</code> with the id the server chose before accepting <code>PlayerAuthInput</code> again. The four-step detour survives only for a swap between two worlds of the same Bedrock id, which the client otherwise never closes.</p><p><strong>Portals in two layers.</strong> Geometry (frame scan, ring match, creation footprint, End platform) is pure functions in <code>packages/block-behaviors/src/rules/portal.ts</code> with <code>portalFamily</code> hooks for lighting, collapse, end ring activation and the piglin random tick. Travel is a <code>DimensionTravel</code> service in <code>packages/prismarine/src/world/portal/</code> with a rules table, per-entity contact timers and cooldown, a <code>PortalIndex</code> per world, scaling by <code>coordinate_scale</code> from the two types, and the wire sequence above.</p><h2 id="alternatives-rejected" tabindex="-1">Alternatives rejected <a class="header-anchor" href="#alternatives-rejected" aria-label="Permalink to “Alternatives rejected”">​</a></h2><p><strong>A, a per-world generator string plus a <code>dimension</code> field.</strong> <code>WorldData.dimension?: &#39;overworld&#39; | &#39;nether&#39; | &#39;the_end&#39;</code>, <code>Nether</code> and <code>TheEnd</code> generator classes, <code>WorldManager</code> loading every <code>worlds.*</code> entry. Three config entries make a three-dimension server and nothing new is parsed. This is Cloudburst&#39;s shape, and it fails the goal in four places. Each dimension is its own folder with its own <code>level.dat</code>, so a vanilla Bedrock save cannot be imported or exported as one world. Generator settings hide in class names, so <code>large_biomes</code>, <code>amplified</code>, a custom noise setting or a flat preset each need a new class. A datapack still cannot add a dimension. A plugin generator still cannot receive settings. The storage note&#39;s section 4.2 and the portal note&#39;s chunk 1 both started from this shape; the dimension note&#39;s section 4.2 is why the orchestrator chose B over it, and this ADR records that choice so the smaller shape is not reintroduced slice by slice.</p><p><strong>C, Bedrock&#39;s add-on <code>dimension/*.json</code> as the primary model.</strong> Preview 26.50.25 added <code>minecraft:dimension_height</code>, <code>minecraft:default_biome</code> and <code>minecraft:generation</code>, whose only generator is <code>void</code>, and the same release stopped packs from redefining vanilla dimensions. It can describe a custom void dimension&#39;s bounds and nothing about terrain, and it cannot describe the three dimensions every server has. It stays open as an input format layered on B: a pack dimension becomes a stem <code>{ type: &lt;synthesized from dimension_height&gt;, generator: { type: &#39;jsprismarine:void&#39; } }</code>.</p><p><strong>A <code>Dimension</code> object inside <code>World</code>, with <code>World</code> as the save.</strong> The storage note&#39;s section 4.3 drew this split: <code>World</code> keeps provider, seed, clock, game rules, weather and the roster, and N <code>Dimension</code> objects own chunks, generator, entities, systems and the change sink, introduced behind forwarders. We reject it for three reasons. First, the numbers point the other way: measured by regex over non-test source, <code>getWorld()</code> appears 102 times in 38 files and the per-dimension methods dominate (<code>setBlock</code> 32, <code>getBlock</code> 29, <code>getPlayers</code> 28, <code>getLoadedChunk</code> 26, <code>getEntities</code> 20, <code>getEntity</code> 20) against the save-level ones (<code>getGameRuleManager</code> 14, <code>getTicks</code> 12, <code>getGameRule</code> 11, <code>weatherCycle</code> 6, <code>getSeed</code> 4). Forwarding the few save-level getters from a per-dimension <code>World</code> to its <code>Level</code> is a handful of one-line methods; forwarding the many per-dimension methods from a save-level <code>World</code> to &quot;the dimension of the position it is handed, or the Overworld when a caller passes none&quot; turns every <code>setBlock</code> into a lookup and makes a missing position silently mean the Overworld. Second, <code>Position</code> carries a <code>World</code> and <code>Entity</code> reaches its server through <code>position.getWorld()</code>, so the object a position names must be the one that holds the entity&#39;s chunks. Third, everything already keyed per dimension (<code>Chunk</code>, the LevelDB keys, <code>WorldChangeSink</code>, the content runtime&#39;s <code>worldFor(dimension)</code>, <code>Server.getEntityRuntime(dimension)</code>) is keyed to <code>World</code> today and keeps working unchanged. Every Bedrock peer surveyed (Dragonfly, PowerNukkitX, Cloudburst, Allay) also has one world object per dimension. What is new is the <code>Level</code> above it, which is small and which nothing addresses by position.</p><p><strong>A new <code>packages/dimensions</code> package.</strong> The parsing half has the same inputs and style as <code>@jsprismarine/worldgen</code>, the runtime half needs prismarine&#39;s <code>Chunk</code> and <code>ServerContent</code>, and the overlay is three rows that belong with the rest of mapping. A separate package would hold types only and add a boundary every other package crosses.</p><p><strong>Three eager worker pools, one per dimension.</strong> The pool is the largest memory consumer in the process. Keying the worker&#39;s pipeline cache by (settings, seed) instead of seed is the smaller change and keeps the worker count at <code>min(6, cores - 2)</code> for the whole server.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><p><code>Generator.generateChunk(cx, cz, seed, config)</code> keeps its signature, as ADR 0002 requires, so the chunk pipeline, the scheduler and the chunk tests are unaffected by who builds the generator.</p><p><code>DimensionDefinition</code> becomes a deprecated alias of <code>DimensionType</code> for one release, and <code>Flat</code> and <code>Overworld</code> stay as exports of <code>Generators.ts</code> for one release as thin wrappers that build through the factories, so <code>registerClassGenerator</code> callers keep compiling. Both go in the release after.</p><p><code>World</code> gains a <code>Level</code> and forwards <code>getTicks</code>, <code>getGameRuleManager</code>, <code>getGameRule</code>, weather and <code>getSeed</code> to it. The 102 <code>getWorld()</code> call sites do not move. Code that iterated <code>WorldManager.getWorlds()</code> to find &quot;the Nether&quot; now asks a <code>Level</code> for <code>world(&#39;minecraft:the_nether&#39;)</code>.</p><p>The provider interface changes shape. <code>readChunk</code> and <code>listChunks</code> take a dimension, which is a breaking change for any out-of-tree provider; <code>@jsprismarine/prismarine</code> is below 1.0.0, so the Changeset bump is <code>minor</code>.</p><p>A LevelDB folder written by this server before the change needs no conversion: Overworld keys carry no dimension integer, exactly as before, and the stems are written on first load from the translated config.</p><p>The config file converges on the new keys. A config with <code>generator: &#39;Flat&#39;</code> and <code>layers</code> reads as <code>preset: &#39;minecraft:flat&#39;</code> with an Overworld stem override; any other generator string is an error naming the registered kinds, as today.</p><p>The worker pool is shared, so a Nether chunk and an Overworld chunk compete for the same six workers. Lazy <code>load</code> means the first portal trip pays the destination&#39;s pipeline build (<code>RandomState</code> costs 55 to 134 ms per settings and seed); <code>load: &#39;eager&#39;</code> moves that to startup.</p><p>The client-e2e suite verifies <code>ChangeDimension</code> to custom id 1000 and arrival chunks with its pinned Cinnabar 1.26.60 client. Mojang&#39;s retail Bedrock client remains unverified. Inventory and effects across a dimension change also remain unverified against a client.</p></div>`,1)]])}var s=t(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};